        <li><a href="/math/volume-calculator/" class="font-bold text-blue-600 hover:underline dark:text-blue-400">Volume Calculator</a> – Compute 3D volumetric capacity for prisms, pyramids, and cylinders.</li>
        <li><a href="/general/percentage-calculator/" class="font-bold text-blue-600 hover:underline dark:text-blue-400">Percentage Calculator</a> – Fast percentage increases, decreases, and proportion solvers.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Frequently Asked Questions Accordions -->
    <div class="space-y-3 pt-6 border-t border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 not-prose">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-2">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800" open>
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            1. How do you calculate the area of a right triangle?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-sm">
          Multiply the lengths of the two perpendicular legs together and divide the result by 2: <code>Area = (a × b) / 2</code>. Because the legs meet at a 90-degree angle, one leg acts as the base and the other acts as the perpendicular height.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            2. How do you find the area if you only know one leg and the hypotenuse?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-sm">
          First use the Pythagorean theorem to calculate the missing leg: <code>b = √(c² − a²)</code>. Once both legs are known, apply the standard formula: <code>Area = (a × b) / 2</code>.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            3. Can I find the area using only the hypotenuse?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-sm">
          Not unless you also know that the triangle is isosceles (45°-45°-90°). For a 45°-45°-90° triangle, the area is <code>Area = c² / 4</code>. In non-isosceles right triangles, infinitely many different combinations of legs can share the exact same hypotenuse, so an additional angle or leg measurement is required.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            4. How do angles help determine the area?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-sm">
          By applying trigonometry: if hypotenuse <em>c</em> and acute angle <em>α</em> are known, the legs are <code>a = c × sin(α)</code> and <code>b = c × cos(α)</code>. The area evaluates to <code>Area = [c² × sin(α) × cos(α)] / 2</code>.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            5. What is the area of a 45°-45°-90° triangle?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-sm">
          In an isosceles right triangle where <em>a = b</em>, the area is <code>Area = a² / 2</code>. Expressed in terms of the hypotenuse <em>c</em>, the area is <code>Area = c² / 4</code>.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            6. How can I check whether three side lengths form a right triangle?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-sm">
          Test the Pythagorean theorem: square the two smaller side lengths and add them together. If the sum exactly equals the square of the longest side (<code>a² + b² = c²</code>), the triangle is guaranteed to be a right triangle.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            7. Why is area measured in square units?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-sm">
          Area measures two-dimensional surface space. When you multiply a length measurement (such as cm) by another perpendicular length measurement (cm), the dimensional unit becomes <code>cm × cm = cm²</code> (square centimeters).
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
